| ''this text'' |            |           |
| ""this text"" |            |           |
+===============+============+===========+


  IV.H. Data Serialization

For data serialization in Fey, we use two specifc data structurs, the list ':'
and the map '#'. These tags can be plain anonymous data, or have their own
name and metadata associated with them. eg. '[name| key value ...]'



  IV.I. Actions

|            |   |   |
+============+===+===+
| [[this]]   |   |   |
| {{this}}   |   |   |
| ((this))   |   |   |
| <<this>>   |   |   |
+============+===+===+
| [[[this]]] |   |   |
| {{{this}}} |   |   |
| (((this))) |   |   |
| <<<this>>> |   |   |
+============+===+===+

$$$  misc
the general idea is that a tag can be one of the following things:
- anonymous tag: `[]`
- anonymous tag with subject: `[: subject]` or `[: some subject]`
- named tag: `[tagname]` or `[tag name]`
- named tag with subject: `[tagname: subject]` or `[tag name: some subject]` etc.


additionally, tags can be given properties based on the group of characters which immediate preced or immediately follow the tag
- heading linking
cannot be anonymous (for now at least it is undefined)
the name should be the filename of the document the heading is in
    - '[]#' - for internal links
    - '#[]' - for external links (experimental. will only look in lateral directories or their subdirectories)
- tag linking
cannot be anonymous (for now at least it is undefined)
the name/subject of the link tag must match exactly the name/subject of the tag it is linking to.
- '[]@' - for internal links
- '@[]' - for external links (experimental. will only look in lateral directories or their subdirectories)
- Monospaced text block
the name of the tags should either match, or the closing tag can be anonymous.
the name of the tags inform the interpreter what language syntax highlighting to use, ie. 'zig', 'c', 'python', etc.
    - '[]{' open monospaced text block
    - '}[]' close monospaced text block

- Line-wise tags
- '[]:' - all of the content up to the next newline is considered the body of the tag
- ':[]' - all of the content preceding the tag on the current line is considered the body of the tag
$$$





